# Repo-sync systemd timer

This document explains the optional systemd timer used to keep the local checkout of the OTP Relay Kubernetes repository synchronized with GitHub.

The repo-sync timer belongs to the operator automation layer. It is intentionally limited to repository synchronization only.

---

## Layer purpose

The repo-sync timer periodically runs:

```text
scripts/sync-repo.sh
```

That script updates the local checkout from `origin/main` and preserves runtime/generated artifacts.

The timer is useful when the server should automatically receive repository file updates without giving GitHub Actions direct deployment control.

---

## Safety boundary

The repo-sync timer does not deploy the application.

It must not run:

```text
setup.sh
install-otp-relay-k8s.sh
ansible-playbook
helm install
helm upgrade
kubectl apply
kubectl rollout restart
k3s installer
Docker image build/import
GitHub runner installation
```

This boundary is intentional.

Repository synchronization and cluster deployment are separate operator actions.

---

## Why this exists

This repository previously used a GitHub Actions runner based workflow. The current model is safer for this environment:

```text
GitHub web edit
  -> repo-sync timer updates local checkout
  -> operator reviews or intentionally runs setup/install
  -> cluster changes happen only during explicit operator-controlled setup
```

This prevents a GitHub push from automatically mutating the live cluster.

---

## Files involved

```text
scripts/sync-repo.sh
  Performs the actual Git fetch/reset/clean operation.

scripts/install-repo-sync-timer.sh
  Installs or updates the systemd service and timer units.

systemd/README-repo-sync-timer
  This documentation file.
```

The generated systemd unit files are written under:

```text
/etc/systemd/system/
```

The exact unit names are created by `scripts/install-repo-sync-timer.sh`.

---

## Preserved paths

The sync script must preserve local runtime/generated artifacts such as:

```text
.env
automation/ansible/inventory.generated.ini
automation/libvirt/build/
dist/
release/
.sync-state/
.venv/
venv/
node_modules/
frontend/help/
frontend/app.js
install-report.txt
```

These paths are not treated as source files managed by Git sync.

Important examples:

```text
frontend/app.jsx is source.
frontend/app.js is generated.

docs/help/ is source.
frontend/help/ is generated.

node_modules/ is generated dependency output.
```

The sync script should not chmod or clean inside generated dependency/output directories.

---

## Installing the timer

From the repository root:

```bash
sudo bash scripts/install-repo-sync-timer.sh
```

The installer should:

```text
validate the repository path
write the systemd service unit
write the systemd timer unit
reload systemd
optionally enable/start the timer
print status and next-run guidance
```

---

## Checking timer status

Use:

```bash
systemctl list-timers | grep -i repo || true
systemctl status otp-relay-repo-sync.timer --no-pager || true
systemctl status otp-relay-repo-sync.service --no-pager || true
```

If the exact unit name changes, check the output printed by `scripts/install-repo-sync-timer.sh`.

---

## Running repo sync manually

Manual sync remains the primary debugging path:

```bash
cd /opt/k8s-ansible
bash scripts/sync-repo.sh
```

Expected safe behavior:

```text
fetch origin/main
compare local HEAD with remote HEAD
reset local checkout only when GitHub changed
clean untracked files while preserving runtime/generated paths
restore executable bits only for repository shell scripts
write sync state
exit without deployment side effects
```

---

## Confirming no deployment happened

After a sync-only run, the cluster should not have changed unless an operator ran setup/install separately.

Useful checks:

```bash
sudo k3s kubectl get pods -n otp-relay -o wide
sudo k3s kubectl get pods -n observability -o wide
sudo k3s kubectl get svc -A | grep -E 'traefik|otp-relay|grafana' || true
```

Repo sync should not restart pods or apply manifests.

---

## Troubleshooting

### Timer ran but files did not update

Check:

```bash
cd /opt/k8s-ansible
git remote -v
git branch --show-current
git rev-parse HEAD
git rev-parse origin/main
bash scripts/sync-repo.sh
```

### Generated files were removed

Check that `scripts/sync-repo.sh` preserves generated/runtime paths and that the running script is the corrected version:

```bash
grep -n 'PRESERVE_PATHS' -A20 scripts/sync-repo.sh
grep -n 'node_modules\|frontend/help\|frontend/app.js' scripts/sync-repo.sh
grep -n 'restore_executable_bits' -A25 scripts/sync-repo.sh
```

### node_modules chmod warnings appear

The sync script is entering generated dependency output and must be fixed.

The corrected behavior is to prune `node_modules` before chmod traversal.

Expected command structure inside `restore_executable_bits()`:

```text
-path "./node_modules" ... -prune
```

### Timer accidentally deploys workloads

Disable it immediately:

```bash
sudo systemctl disable --now otp-relay-repo-sync.timer || true
sudo systemctl disable --now otp-relay-repo-sync.service || true
```

Then inspect the generated service unit. It should call only:

```text
scripts/sync-repo.sh
```

It should not call setup, installer, Ansible, Helm, kubectl apply, Docker build, or K3s installation commands.

---

## Design note

This timer is intentionally different from a GitHub Actions deployment runner.

The timer keeps local files current. It does not decide when production should change.

The operator remains responsible for explicitly running setup or install when a synchronized repository change should be applied to the cluster.
